  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nOur weather has returned to a mild & dry look thanks to the \ncombination of a sprawling surface high and upper level ridging \nacross the eastern CONUS. High temperatures will be in the mid to \nupper 70s Tuesday with lows in the upper 50s to lower 60s. Our next \nsystem arrives on Wednesday into Wednesday night that will bring a \nmixture of strong winds in the mountains and foothills and another \nround of beneficial rainfall across the region. Winds will begin to \nincrease Tuesday night and peak on Wednesday with gusts up to \nadvisory level possible. \n\nThe cold front passage will bring a round of showers with a few \nthunderstorms possible Wednesday into Wednesday night. Severe \nweather is not expected. Forecast soundings show limited instability \n(50-150 J/Kg) despite sufficient shear (30+ knots) present. This is \nnot enough to get meaningful convection development outside of \nthunder chances. There will be a good surge of moisture associated \nwith this system as southerly flow advects higher PW air into the \nregion over the next 48 hours. QPF amounts generally range between \n0.25\" to 0.75\" outside of a few locations with locally higher \nvalues. At this point though any rainfall is beneficial given how \ndry the last 4 to 6 weeks have been. Our system and front will exit \nthe area on Thursday leaving behind northwest flow and cold air \nadvection. This will keep afternoon PoPs present on Thursday with \nisolated showers possible. Temperatures will begin to cool as we \nhead into the weekend, but the cold air advection isn't that cold. \nWe'll ultimately end up with seasonable temperatures with highs in \nthe 60s and lows in the 30s and 40s Thursday through the weekend. \nWinds will taper off on Thursday as the mountain wave set up \nresolves. The drier, cooler airmass will lead to a rather pleasant \nweekend with mostly sunny skies and comfortable temperatures. \n\nLooking at the end of the period, there will be another shortwave \ntrough that will drop out of the Great Lakes/Upper Midwest Sunday \nnight into Monday that will support a chance of precipitation.",
